## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical control of network addressing across environments, Terraform input validation, and CI gating to prevent risk without applying changes.

## Key Concepts
- CIDR overlap validation in Terraform
- Module input validation
- CI gates using plan
- Multi-env network design

## Code Example
```terraform
variable "environment_cidrs" {
  type = map(string)
  validation {
    condition = alltrue([
      for env, cidr in var.environment_cidrs : cidr != "" && !contains(var.allocated_cidrs, cidr)
    ])
    error_message = "CIDR overlaps with allocated CIDRs or is empty"
  }
}
variable "allocated_cidrs" {
  type = set(string)
  description = "CIDRs already allocated to prod/staging"
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to handle dynamic CIDR allocation across environments?
- How would you handle updates to the allocated CIDR store without causing plan drift?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to compose remote state across envs, leverage backend aliases, and isolate region-specific DNS resources. Also assesses practical CI gating to prevent prod drift.

## Key Concepts
- Backend aliases and per-env state
- terraform_remote_state data source for cross-env outputs
- for_each with a DNS map to generate records
- CI gating with plan JSON to block prod changes

## Code Example
```hcl
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "tfstate-bucket"
    key    = "envs/region-eu/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "eu-west-1"
  }
}

data "terraform_remote_state" "prod" {
  backend = "s3"
  config = {
    bucket = "tfstate-bucket"
    key    = "envs/prod/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}

module "dns" {
  source       = "./modules/dns"
  dns_records  = var.dns_records
  prod_zone_id = data.terraform_remote_state.prod.outputs.zone_id
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this to 5 regions while keeping drift detection?
- How do you handle secrets in backend configs for multi-tenant regions?
